#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Put a recorded mini4pro flight on a live Zenoh bus, at the speed it happened.

A black box replay: hand it a flight record and it goes on the bus, on the key expressions
`docs/zenoh-topics.md` specifies, with the recorded wall-clock spacing — so DiMOS can be
developed against a real flight with no aircraft, no phone and no Android build. Encoding
happens on the way past by running `zenohreplay`, which is where every encoding decision
lives; a frames file works too, for when you want to inspect the encoding rather than send it.

    tools/zenohpublish flight.jsonl                   # to the router on hyper1
    tools/zenohpublish flight.jsonl --local           # laptop only, nothing else running
    tools/zenohpublish flight.jsonl --speed 10 --loop # a fast, endless drone
    tools/zenohpublish flight.jsonl --start 60 --end 120  # just the orbit

    tools/zenohpublish --spy                          # subscribe, decode, and say so
    tools/zenohpublish f.jsonl --selftest             # publish->subscribe->decode, in one process
    tools/zenohpublish --keys                         # the contract, printed

What a consumer needs to know before reading the output:

- **The gaps are the point.** The default is the recorded spacing, gaps included; `odom` was
  withheld 78% of the time on this airframe and a replay that smoothed that over would be lying
  about the hardest thing a consumer has to cope with. `--speed` scales the timeline, it does
  not fill it in.
- **Nothing is re-encoded.** A frame goes out exactly as `zenohreplay` encoded it, which is
  byte-for-byte what the Kotlin encoder produced for the same flight. `--restamp` is the one
  exception and says so on the tin.
- **The frames are `drone/`-namespaced and there is no `datum` channel** — the origin is
  recovered by subtracting `pose` from `gps_location`.

This is a contract, not a script: the Android publisher replaces it in the live path and must
produce the same stream. Full rationale: `docs/zenoh-replay-contract.md` (`--keys` prints §1).
"""

